A||N||3||David Johnson||Power windows||||5||
Z||000000||David Johnson||03-31-2000||10:29 AM||||My 87 GT Power window on the passenger side hasn't worked for 3 years. I have read everything posted in the past and taken the door apart twice. I can't see the motor or any of the other components.  Can you get to it from the outside by taking off the plastic door panel? is there a good tutorial with pictures somewhere?<BR>David||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||03-31-2000||11:20 AM||||It'll be a little easier to see from the outside, but not much.  Sounds like you need a new motor?  2 part rivets.  Drill out the center of the rivet, then use a chisel to get the main/outer head off.  At least that's what I did.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||03-31-2000||03:16 PM||||Probably not the problem, but my passenger side window stopped working due to a wire (black) coming undone from under the switch.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-31-2000||09:58 PM||||This is how I used to test door motors to make sure it was the motor or a wire problem.<P>if you can reach it from inside then great. otherwise take off the skin.<P>then test the motor direct.<P><B>WARNING:</B> make sure your hands and the test leads are clear of the window machinery durring testing!<P>disconnect the door motor from the wiring. I think there is a connector in the door near the motor. that's where you want to connect.<P>get some push on terminals that fit the dor motor connector. put these on 2 12 gauge pieces of wire. <P>In one lead install a 30 amp fuse. <B>This is critical. you can start a fire if you don't do this.</B> you can get a fuse holder when you get the terminals.<P>get a charged car battery and put it on the ground by the door. connect one wire to the negative post with a small hose clamp. gickly tap the other on the +. if the window didn't move reverse the leads and try again. DO NOT hold the lead on the battery for more than a second. once you have the polarity figured out then you can test travel if need be.||||reg||1
